t also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/269d503189ab1027c3d8028542ef0c6a0e2f146aef0f7916691428866ce1b894.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120771" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-120771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120771" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the general solution to the equation $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=0?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-120777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({15}^{∘})=2-\\sqrt{3}.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(2-\\sqrt{3})={15}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323833" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-76741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})=1+\\sqrt{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-76741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(\\sqrt{2}+1)=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-76741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-76741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-76741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-76741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-{60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-16229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323836" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-1$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing this as $x=\\theta +{180}^{∘}\\,n,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "135"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-323846" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-29979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/355d3770234a7b0c9a65965d65ad3807adedc7e9c6c8a23ddbe25bb34a0dd5d0.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-29979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.$ \n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-29979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-29979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-29979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-29979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/350458d29c8d214f44b175e28a6f43accd7ce064187cf755324cbe99d53eb7ad.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $y=-1.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-1.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-16245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi "] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/f6e3ee6eed44a5ecb7e037e9cd8401a95b3e2a4640bd4454f60019f5bd9d1e67.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-16246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-16246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-324056" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c27398934832fba39571fb74c9fa2f9ef80d5ff8ea27136f5353a67ea2cd9ae6.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c78678fade9fd2a60003a0ab2cac0299ef1e7df9f6633b1fc6a97422e13dfc4d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "987f404ca61dd5e1564c1e3cf4654c143a9fdae95d95829181389d88f8fff849"} {"question" "q-120791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "q-120791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d9e5c26a5f674a27efbbd96ae3747c48b46eaa263ccb97fea15be94f29dd07"} {"question" "e-3552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to $2\\cos{x} + \\sqrt{2}= 0,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3552.json" "file_sha256" "880102df955d6b30a33daff08f669434be3b2cb9645946adf552b01fe167d7ef"} {"question" "e-3552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cos x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos\\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x<   0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/5f32529ce4132c594d8a886222ff6f1a356eefa95fa4c7e1fd38b9bd4e3932a9.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in[0,360)$ are $x=135^\\circ, 225^\\circ.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is\n\n$$\nx = 135^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=225^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3552.json" "file_sha256" "880102df955d6b30a33daff08f669434be3b2cb9645946adf552b01fe167d7ef"} {"question" "e-3554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the general solution to the equation $3\\csc x+2\\sqrt{3}=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3554.json" "file_sha256" "cb7adee1b324d9c725583ede94b3d49ce704a162606e90da3f23d1c7b4648a9d"} {"question" "e-3554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\csc x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =-\\dfrac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\sin x<   0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/0716b8ab50e525f453ae8bc690d404a150f2da98f0a90b6c8856ae9925e7c515.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in[0,2\\pi)$ are $x=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3554.json" "file_sha256" "cb7adee1b324d9c725583ede94b3d49ce704a162606e90da3f23d1c7b4648a9d"} {"question" "e-3553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the general solution to the equation $\\cot{x}=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3553.json" "file_sha256" "d7fc12be25b89e90b518baa98b64d17cdc52344be2696913cb0c7028a18040a1"} {"question" "e-3553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arctan \\left( 1 \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi.$  Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is: \n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3553.json" "file_sha256" "d7fc12be25b89e90b518baa98b64d17cdc52344be2696913cb0c7028a18040a1"} {"question" "e-3555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\sec{x}$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ What is the general solution for the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/cae8d73bdf9a21e956c444db4b22bfc26e20b7a9d1593d7759fc119aa4c6a623.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3555.json" "file_sha256" "354dd5a236c5ef7544d8928de06231beb977fdb8c851b0ab49fac5a231c40742"} {"question" "e-3555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\sec x =\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nRecall that $\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x >   0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in\\left[0^\\circ,360^\\circ\\right)$ are $x=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = 45^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ , \\quad x=315^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/example-3555.json" "file_sha256" "354dd5a236c5ef7544d8928de06231beb977fdb8c851b0ab49fac5a231c40742"}] "usage" {"e-3552" [] "e-3555" [] "e-3554" [] "q-16229" [] "e-3553" []} "basis" 843 "retractions" [[17592186101147 :question/problem "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\tan x =-\\sqrt{3}$. Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186100566 :question/problem "Find the general solution to $2\\cos{x} + \\sqrt{2}= 0$, where $x$ is measured in degrees."] [17592186100566 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cos x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x + \\sqrt{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos x &= -\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/8584042d6ef9bb2998a7c62ae2b7e609.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 180^{∘} + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in[0,360)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is\n\n$$\nx = 135^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=225^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ\n$$"] [17592186100568 :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $3\\csc x+2\\sqrt{3}=0$?"] [17592186100568 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\csc x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\csc x + 2\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\csc x &= -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\csc x &= -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =-\\dfrac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_{1} = \\arcsin (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = \\frac{5π}{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin x< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/55452604d3a171e9228cc769aae317ea.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in[0,2\\pi)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."] [17592186100567 :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\cot{x}=1$?"] [17592186100567 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan x &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arctan \\left(1 \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi$. Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is:\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."] [17592186100569 :question/problem "The graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\sec{x}$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2}$. What is the general solution for the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/42b069cebd215708e6a018e0e3ecc90d.png)"] [17592186100569 :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x =\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} &= \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x > 0$, the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/a8b3e42e6690c60434773d04d3b5d73d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 315^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in\\left[0^\\circ,360^\\circ\\right)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = 45^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=315^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098653 17592186098654 17592186098655 17592186098656 17592186098657] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098652 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098652 :answer-field/id] [17592186098652 :answer-field/key] [17592186098652 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
